## Shared machine: leasehold

This machine is shared, and leasehold decides who runs heavy work when. Builds, test suites
and container builds take a lease of some slots; when the machine is full they wait their turn
instead of slowing everyone down.

- Before heavy work, `leasehold status` shows how busy the machine is and who is waiting. When
  it is full, do light work (read, edit, write tests) first.
- Run heavy commands as `leasehold hold --weight N -- <command>`, unless this machine's shims
  or agent hooks already do it for you (`leasehold commands --test "<command>"` says whether
  a command takes a lease, and how heavy it is).
- Exit 75 means the machine stayed busy until the timeout: retry later. Exit 77 means the
  request can never fit: ask for a smaller weight. Exit 69 means no daemon: run without it.
- A last line `leasehold: ... This is not a test failure.` means memory ran out and the
  kernel killed the command; its tests did not fail. Run it later or with fewer workers, not
  unchanged.
- Never run a heavy command a way that avoids the lease (another name, a full path, a
  wrapper): it only makes everyone's work slower, yours included.
